window.docContents[221].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--From-the-present-theory-to-broader-elliptic-analysis":{"contents":"The main open extensions are:\n\n* Green representation on domains with boundary regularity between \nand ;* operational atlases from geometric boundary hypotheses other than local\n graphs with , in particular  boundaries;* bounded  solution operators for full lower-order problems\nin their whole uniquely solvable range, below the Gårding shift;* Neumann and mixed boundary-value theories, and nonhomogeneous Dirichlet\nregularity beyond the proved Poisson examples and supplied Sobolev boundary classes;* general  estimates and Schauder theory; and* Weyl-type eigenvalue asymptotics and finer spectral estimates.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems","header":"5.7.1.4. From the present theory to broader elliptic analysis","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--From-the-present-theory-to-broader-elliptic-analysis"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Contact-with-an-obstacle":{"contents":"An inequality constraint changes the Euler equation precisely where the\nminimizer touches the obstacle. Consider\n\n\n\nUpward variations are admissible at contact, while downward ones may not be.\nThe resulting reaction  is nonnegative. Away from contact\nboth directions become available and the Poisson equation is recovered.\nStarting from a constrained minimizer, the regularity theorem supplies the\nextra differentiability needed to express this description almost everywhere.\n\nLet , , be bounded and open with \ngraph boundary. Let  and let the obstacle  be\n up to the boundary, continuous on , and nonpositive\non . If  minimizes  among fields\nabove , then  has an  representative . Its reaction obeysOn the contact set , one has  almost\neverywhere; on , one has  almost everywhere.\n\nThe graph boundary supplies the extension and trace infrastructure.\nThe classical obstacle defines an  field whose Sobolev trace agrees\nwith its boundary values, so the boundary sign becomes the sign condition\nneeded by the obstacle regularity theorem. The constrained variational\ninequality and its reaction estimate then give the  lift and\n. Complementarity sets the reaction to zero\noff contact. On contact, the Sobolev derivatives of  vanish\nalmost everywhere on its zero set, giving equality of the Laplacians.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Null Lagrangians and Constraints","header":"9.3.3.4. Contact with an obstacle","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Contact-with-an-obstacle"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Separation-of-Variables-and-Spectral-Expansions--Separation-of-variables--Abstract-spectral-expansions":{"contents":"A single separated mode solves the equation but matches only its own initial\ndatum. Representing general data requires expanding in the whole family\n. Here that family is an arbitrary Hilbert basis of an inner\nproduct space  over  or .\n\nFor any Hilbert basis  and any vector , the Fourier coefficients\n reconstruct u: the net of finite sums\n converges to u in the Hilbert norm. This\nis Mathlib's basis theorem behind Evans's eigenfunction-series method.\n\nMathlib defines a Hilbert basis by its coefficient map, a linear isometric\nequivalence onto the square-summable sequences; it sends  to the sequence\nof its coefficients. Such a sequence is the sum, in the sequence norm, of its\nsingle-entry pieces. The inverse isometry is continuous and maps each piece to\nthe coefficient times the basis vector in , so the expansion converges to .\n\nThe expansion converges as a net indexed by finite subsets of the index set.\nSpectral methods truncate an -indexed basis to the initial\nsegments , and this unconditional convergence covers them in\nparticular.\n\nFor a basis indexed by the natural numbers, the conventional first-N\npartial sums converge in norm:\n\nThe basis expansion gives convergence of the net over all finite index sets.\nThe initial segments  eventually contain every fixed finite\nset. Restricting the convergent net to these initial segments gives\n in norm.\n\nNorm convergence of the expansion is the qualitative statement; Parseval's\nidentity is its quantitative counterpart, obtained by taking the squared norm\non both sides and using orthonormality to remove the cross terms. The\ncoefficients are written  with the basis vector in the\nfirst slot, following Mathlib's convention for which argument is\nconjugate-linear.\n\nThe squared Hilbert norm is exactly the sum of the squared norms of the\nHilbert-basis coefficients:\n\nMathlib's coefficient map is a linear isometry onto the square-summable\nsequences, and the coefficient of each index is the inner product with that\nbasis vector. The squared norm of a square-summable sequence is the sum of the\nsquared norms of its entries. Applying this to the coefficient sequence and\nusing that the isometry preserves norms yields Parseval's identity.\n\nSubtracting the first  terms from  leaves a vector whose nonzero\ncoefficients are exactly the omitted ones, so Parseval applied to that\ndifference converts the truncation error into a coefficient sum.\n\nThe squared error of the first-N approximation is exactly the sum of the\nsquared omitted coefficients:This is the quantitative identity behind\nspectral truncation and Galerkin approximation estimates.\n\nSplit the series of squared coefficient norms after its first  terms; the\ntail is indexed by . Orthonormality makes the squared truncation error\nequal to the squared norm minus the retained squared coefficients, and Parseval\nidentifies the squared norm with the full series. Hence, with ,\nthe error equals the tail, which is the displayed formula.\n\nThe tail identity by itself gives no rate, since it only rearranges the\ncoefficients. A rate appears once the coefficients are known to decay: if\n is finite, then every\nomitted index satisfies , so the monotone weight  can be\npulled out of the tail and compared termwise with the weighted energy. For\neigenfunction expansions such weighted summability is a regularity statement\nabout , expressed spectrally.\n\nFor , if the coefficient energy weighted by  is summable, then the\nfirst-N squared truncation error is bounded by that weighted energy divided\nby .\n\nMultiply the exact omitted tail by the monotone weight at index N, compare\nit termwise with the weighted tail, and divide by the strictly positive\nquantity .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Separation of Variables and Spectral Expansions\u0009Separation of variables","header":"9.2.1.3.1.1. Abstract spectral expansions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Separation-of-Variables-and-Spectral-Expansions--Separation-of-variables--Abstract-spectral-expansions"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models":{"contents":"PDEAtlas uses two complementary analytic models.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.1. The two Sobolev models","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models"}});